    "description": "Creatine occupies a defined place in human biochemistry. Creatine is a nitrogen-containing compound that participates in the phosphocreatine system, buffering ATP demand during rapid changes in cellular energy use, especially in skeletal muscle and brain.\n\nThe reason it appears in a pharmacology-oriented database is its connection with Phosphocreatine Resynthesis, Creatine kinase system, and Phosphocreatine shuttle. In sports physiology, it is mainly discussed in relation to exercise capacity, substrate availability, training adaptation or recovery. This is also why formulation is not a cosmetic detail: Common forms include Monohydrate, HCl, Citrate, Kre-Alkalyn.\n\nTypical intake: 3–5 g/day of creatine monohydrate is the standard maintenance range; loading protocols commonly use ~20 g/day split into several doses for 5–7 days.\n\nFor study purposes, it is useful to separate four questions. First, what chemical species actually reaches the body? Second, what molecular process can it influence? Third, which biomarker should move if that process changes? Fourth, does that biomarker movement translate into a meaningful physiological outcome? The linked mechanism and biomarker records on this site are designed around exactly that chain.\n\nLater evidence reviews can therefore add studies without rewriting the underlying educational description of Creatine.",
