    "description": "L-Tyrosine is best understood as a specific chemical entity rather than as a generic “supplement”. Tyrosine is an amino-acid precursor for catecholamines. It supplies substrate for dopamine and, downstream, norepinephrine and epinephrine synthesis. In neuropharmacology, it is mainly discussed in relation to arousal, attention, neurotransmitter synthesis, synaptic signaling or neuronal energy metabolism.\n\nThe main biological connections currently mapped for this entry are Catecholamine synthesis and Dopamine synthesis. In practical terms, that means the compound can be studied at several levels: molecular target, pathway activity, downstream biomarker and finally a physiological or clinical outcome. Keeping those levels separate is particularly useful for pharmacology students because a change in one pathway does not automatically imply a change in every downstream endpoint.\n\nForm and dose matter. Common forms include L-Tyrosine. Typical intake: Acute cognitive/stress studies often use roughly 100–150 mg/kg before a stressor; everyday supplemental products more commonly provide ~500–2,000 mg per serving.\n\nThe database therefore connects L-Tyrosine to its mechanisms and biomarkers rather than treating it as an isolated product. That structure makes it possible to compare different forms of the same substance and later attach primary studies to the exact claim being made.",
