    "description": "What is it? Creatine is a nitrogen-containing compound that participates in the phosphocreatine system, buffering ATP demand during rapid changes in cellular energy use, especially in skeletal muscle and brain.\n\nWhat is it used for? In sports physiology, it is mainly discussed in relation to exercise capacity, substrate availability, training adaptation or recovery. The current pathway map connects Creatine Monohydrate with Phosphocreatine Resynthesis, Creatine kinase system, and Phosphocreatine shuttle. For a beginner in pharmacology, these links are useful because they show where an ingredient sits in the chain from molecular interaction to measurable physiology.\n\nHow is it normally taken? Common creatine protocols use about 3–5 g/day of creatine, with some loading protocols using ~20 g/day divided across several doses for 5–7 days. Common forms include Creatine Monohydrate.\n\nThe exact preparation matters: an isolated molecule, a salt, a standardized botanical extract and a multi-ingredient blend should not be treated as equivalent simply because their labels use similar names. The same principle applies to dose: the amount used in a mechanistic experiment may be very different from the amount used in a human supplementation study.\n\nIn SynergyStacks, the ingredient record is intended to be read together with its linked pathways and biomarkers, giving a compact pharmacology map rather than a one-line supplement description.",
