    "description": "Vitamin C is a vitamin or vitamin-derived compound involved in enzyme reactions, gene regulation, membrane biology or other essential biochemical processes. The exact role depends on its chemical form and whether it is being discussed as a nutrient, a metabolite or a pharmacological supplement.\n\nA useful pharmacology map for Vitamin C starts with its principal biological associations: Collagen Synthesis and Nrf2 / Antioxidant Response. In nutritional pharmacology, its importance is often clearest when intake is inadequate, because correcting a deficiency can normalize enzyme activity, signaling or tissue function. Those associations place the ingredient in the wider network of metabolism, signaling and measurable physiology rather than presenting it as a standalone claim.\n\nTypical intake: Typical supplemental amounts are about 200–1,000 mg/day; nutritional requirements are much lower than many supplemental doses.\n\nCommon forms include Vitamin C. Different salts, extracts, delivery systems and combination products can change the amount of active material that reaches the relevant tissue, so the named ingredient should always be read together with its formulation.\n\nFor students, the most useful way to read this record is as a chain: ingredient → mechanism → biomarker → outcome. The database links are intended to make those relationships visible and provide a place where primary research can later be attached to each step.",
