    "evidenceLevel": "insufficient",
    "description": "Choline occupies a defined place in human biochemistry. Choline is a vitamin or vitamin-derived compound involved in enzyme reactions, gene regulation, membrane biology or other essential biochemical processes. The exact role depends on its chemical form and whether it is being discussed as a nutrient, a metabolite or a pharmacological supplement.\n\nThe reason it appears in a pharmacology-oriented database is its connection with Choline metabolism, Acetylcholine synthesis, and Phosphatidylcholine synthesis. In nutritional pharmacology, its importance is often clearest when intake is inadequate, because correcting a deficiency can normalize enzyme activity, signaling or tissue function. This is also why formulation is not a cosmetic detail: Common forms include Choline.\n\nTypical intake: Supplement products commonly provide 250–1,000 mg/day of choline, depending on the salt/form.\n\nFor study purposes, it is useful to separate four questions. First, what chemical species actually reaches the body? Second, what molecular process can it influence? Third, which biomarker should move if that process changes? Fourth, does that biomarker movement translate into a meaningful physiological outcome? The linked mechanism and biomarker records on this site are designed around exactly that chain.\n\nLater evidence reviews can therefore add studies without rewriting the underlying educational description of Choline.",
