    "evidenceLevel": "emerging",
    "description": "What is it? Iodine is a mineral or mineral salt that supplies an element used in enzymes, structural tissues, membrane potentials or endocrine regulation. The physiological effect depends on the elemental content and chemical form rather than the compound name alone.\n\nWhat is it used for? In nutritional pharmacology, its importance is often clearest when intake is inadequate, because correcting a deficiency can normalize enzyme activity, signaling or tissue function. The current pathway map connects Iodine with Thyroid Hormone Synthesis. For a beginner in pharmacology, these links are useful because they show where an ingredient sits in the chain from molecular interaction to measurable physiology.\n\nHow is it normally taken? Common nutritional supplements provide about 75–150 µg/day; higher amounts are indication-specific. Common forms include Salt or chelate form varies.\n\nThe exact preparation matters: an isolated molecule, a salt, a standardized botanical extract and a multi-ingredient blend should not be treated as equivalent simply because their labels use similar names. The same principle applies to dose: the amount used in a mechanistic experiment may be very different from the amount used in a human supplementation study.\n\nIn SynergyStacks, the ingredient record is intended to be read together with its linked pathways and biomarkers, giving a compact pharmacology map rather than a one-line supplement description.",
