    "description": "Sodium Citrate is a mineral or mineral salt that supplies an element used in enzymes, structural tissues, membrane potentials or endocrine regulation. The physiological effect depends on the elemental content and chemical form rather than the compound name alone.\n\nIn nutritional pharmacology, its importance is often clearest when intake is inadequate, because correcting a deficiency can normalize enzyme activity, signaling or tissue function. Common forms include Salt or chelate form varies. At the biochemical level, the entry is linked to Bone Mineralization, ATP Production, and Insulin Sensitivity. These pathways describe the biological processes in which the compound participates; they are not interchangeable with a measured clinical outcome.\n\nWhy it is used: the most common reason to study or supplement Sodium Citrate is to influence the physiology associated with its chemical role, especially bone mineralization, atp production, and insulin sensitivity. The useful question is therefore not simply whether the ingredient “works”, but which tissue, pathway and measurable outcome are being discussed.\n\nTypical intake: Minerals are best compared by elemental amount. Typical supplemental doses range from roughly one-half to several times the daily nutritional requirement depending on the mineral and its chemical form.\n\nThis page treats Sodium Citrate as a pharmacology reference entry: chemical identity, formulation, biological role and dose are kept separate so that later research citations can be attached to individual claims rather than to a vague overall statement.",
