    "evidenceLevel": "insufficient",
    "description": "Sodium Phosphate is a mineral or mineral salt that supplies an element used in enzymes, structural tissues, membrane potentials or endocrine regulation. The physiological effect depends on the elemental content and chemical form rather than the compound name alone.\n\nA useful pharmacology map for Sodium Phosphate starts with its principal biological associations: Bone Mineralization, ATP Production, and Insulin Sensitivity. In nutritional pharmacology, its importance is often clearest when intake is inadequate, because correcting a deficiency can normalize enzyme activity, signaling or tissue function. Those associations place the ingredient in the wider network of metabolism, signaling and measurable physiology rather than presenting it as a standalone claim.\n\nTypical intake: Minerals are best compared by elemental amount. Typical supplemental doses range from roughly one-half to several times the daily nutritional requirement depending on the mineral and its chemical form.\n\nCommon forms include Salt or chelate form varies. Different salts, extracts, delivery systems and combination products can change the amount of active material that reaches the relevant tissue, so the named ingredient should always be read together with its formulation.\n\nFor students, the most useful way to read this record is as a chain: ingredient → mechanism → biomarker → outcome. The database links are intended to make those relationships visible and provide a place where primary research can later be attached to each step.",
