    "description": "Fish Oil is a lipid or fatty-acid preparation. Such compounds influence membrane composition, energy storage and lipid-derived signaling molecules, with the exact biology determined by chain length, saturation and tissue distribution.\n\nIts most relevant biological context in this database is nf-κb signaling modulation and membrane fluidity. Common forms include Oil or lipid preparation; composition varies. At the biochemical level, the entry is linked to NF-κB Signaling Modulation and Membrane Fluidity. These pathways describe the biological processes in which the compound participates; they are not interchangeable with a measured clinical outcome.\n\nWhy it is used: the most common reason to study or supplement Fish Oil is to influence the physiology associated with its chemical role, especially nf-κb signaling modulation and membrane fluidity. The useful question is therefore not simply whether the ingredient “works”, but which tissue, pathway and measurable outcome are being discussed.\n\nTypical intake: Common supplemental use is measured in grams of fatty acids per day. EPA/DHA products often provide about 1–2 g/day combined, while other fatty-acid preparations use different targets.\n\nThis page treats Fish Oil as a pharmacology reference entry: chemical identity, formulation, biological role and dose are kept separate so that later research citations can be attached to individual claims rather than to a vague overall statement.",
