    "description": "What is it? Algal Oil is a lipid or fatty-acid preparation. Such compounds influence membrane composition, energy storage and lipid-derived signaling molecules, with the exact biology determined by chain length, saturation and tissue distribution.\n\nWhat is it used for? Its most relevant biological context in this database is nf-κb signaling modulation and membrane fluidity. The current pathway map connects Algal Oil with NF-κB Signaling Modulation and Membrane Fluidity. For a beginner in pharmacology, these links are useful because they show where an ingredient sits in the chain from molecular interaction to measurable physiology.\n\nHow is it normally taken? Common supplemental use is measured in grams of fatty acids per day. EPA/DHA products often provide about 1–2 g/day combined, while other fatty-acid preparations use different targets. Common forms include Oil or lipid preparation; composition varies.\n\nThe exact preparation matters: an isolated molecule, a salt, a standardized botanical extract and a multi-ingredient blend should not be treated as equivalent simply because their labels use similar names. The same principle applies to dose: the amount used in a mechanistic experiment may be very different from the amount used in a human supplementation study.\n\nIn SynergyStacks, the ingredient record is intended to be read together with its linked pathways and biomarkers, giving a compact pharmacology map rather than a one-line supplement description.",
