    "evidenceLevel": "emerging",
    "description": "MCT Oil occupies a defined place in human biochemistry. MCT Oil is a lipid or fatty-acid preparation. Such compounds influence membrane composition, energy storage and lipid-derived signaling molecules, with the exact biology determined by chain length, saturation and tissue distribution.\n\nThe reason it appears in a pharmacology-oriented database is its connection with ATP Production. Its most relevant biological context in this database is atp production. This is also why formulation is not a cosmetic detail: Common forms include Oil or lipid preparation; composition varies.\n\nTypical intake: Common supplemental use is measured in grams of fatty acids per day. EPA/DHA products often provide about 1–2 g/day combined, while other fatty-acid preparations use different targets.\n\nFor study purposes, it is useful to separate four questions. First, what chemical species actually reaches the body? Second, what molecular process can it influence? Third, which biomarker should move if that process changes? Fourth, does that biomarker movement translate into a meaningful physiological outcome? The linked mechanism and biomarker records on this site are designed around exactly that chain.\n\nLater evidence reviews can therefore add studies without rewriting the underlying educational description of MCT Oil.",
