    "description": "Perilla Oil is a lipid or fatty-acid preparation. Such compounds influence membrane composition, energy storage and lipid-derived signaling molecules, with the exact biology determined by chain length, saturation and tissue distribution.\n\nA useful pharmacology map for Perilla Oil starts with its principal biological associations: NF-κB Signaling Modulation and Membrane Fluidity. Its most relevant biological context in this database is nf-κb signaling modulation and membrane fluidity. Those associations place the ingredient in the wider network of metabolism, signaling and measurable physiology rather than presenting it as a standalone claim.\n\nTypical intake: Common supplemental use is measured in grams of fatty acids per day. EPA/DHA products often provide about 1–2 g/day combined, while other fatty-acid preparations use different targets.\n\nCommon forms include Oil or lipid preparation; composition varies. Different salts, extracts, delivery systems and combination products can change the amount of active material that reaches the relevant tissue, so the named ingredient should always be read together with its formulation.\n\nFor students, the most useful way to read this record is as a chain: ingredient → mechanism → biomarker → outcome. The database links are intended to make those relationships visible and provide a place where primary research can later be attached to each step.",
