    "description": "Omega-3 Phospholipids is best understood as a specific chemical entity rather than as a generic “supplement”. Omega-3 refers mainly to the long-chain polyunsaturated fatty acids EPA and DHA, which are incorporated into cell membranes and serve as precursors for lipid mediators. Its most relevant biological context in this database is nf-κb signaling modulation and membrane fluidity.\n\nThe main biological connections currently mapped for this entry are NF-κB Signaling Modulation and Membrane Fluidity. In practical terms, that means the compound can be studied at several levels: molecular target, pathway activity, downstream biomarker and finally a physiological or clinical outcome. Keeping those levels separate is particularly useful for pharmacology students because a change in one pathway does not automatically imply a change in every downstream endpoint.\n\nForm and dose matter. Common forms include Oil or lipid preparation; composition varies. Typical intake: Common supplemental use is measured in grams of fatty acids per day. EPA/DHA products often provide about 1–2 g/day combined, while other fatty-acid preparations use different targets.\n\nThe database therefore connects Omega-3 Phospholipids to its mechanisms and biomarkers rather than treating it as an isolated product. That structure makes it possible to compare different forms of the same substance and later attach primary studies to the exact claim being made.",
