    "description": "What is it? Caffeine is a bioactive compound or formulated ingredient used to study or influence a defined physiological process. Its effects depend on its molecular identity, formulation, exposure and the biological system in which it is evaluated.\n\nWhat is it used for? In neuropharmacology, it is mainly discussed in relation to arousal, attention, neurotransmitter synthesis, synaptic signaling or neuronal energy metabolism. The current pathway map connects Caffeine with Adenosine Antagonism. For a beginner in pharmacology, these links are useful because they show where an ingredient sits in the chain from molecular interaction to measurable physiology.\n\nHow is it normally taken? A typical performance dose is ~1–3 mg/kg; stronger ergogenic protocols often use ~3–6 mg/kg taken before exercise. Common forms include Anhydrous, Citrate, Coffee or tea-derived.\n\nThe exact preparation matters: an isolated molecule, a salt, a standardized botanical extract and a multi-ingredient blend should not be treated as equivalent simply because their labels use similar names. The same principle applies to dose: the amount used in a mechanistic experiment may be very different from the amount used in a human supplementation study.\n\nIn SynergyStacks, the ingredient record is intended to be read together with its linked pathways and biomarkers, giving a compact pharmacology map rather than a one-line supplement description.",
