    "evidenceLevel": "insufficient",
    "description": "Inulin is a carbohydrate or fiber substrate that is incompletely digested in the small intestine and can therefore influence stool properties, fermentation and host–microbiome signaling.\n\nIn gastrointestinal physiology, it is mainly discussed through digestion, fermentation, intestinal barrier function and host–microbiome interactions. Common forms include Whole herb or extract; standardization varies. At the biochemical level, the entry is linked to Microbiome Modulation. These pathways describe the biological processes in which the compound participates; they are not interchangeable with a measured clinical outcome.\n\nWhy it is used: the most common reason to study or supplement Inulin is to influence the physiology associated with its chemical role, especially microbiome modulation. The useful question is therefore not simply whether the ingredient “works”, but which tissue, pathway and measurable outcome are being discussed.\n\nTypical intake: There is no single universal dose for this preparation. Human studies use formulation-specific amounts, and the relevant quantity is usually reported for the exact chemical form or extract rather than for the ingredient name alone.\n\nThis page treats Inulin as a pharmacology reference entry: chemical identity, formulation, biological role and dose are kept separate so that later research citations can be attached to individual claims rather than to a vague overall statement.",
