    "description": "What is it? Artichoke Extract is a botanical preparation derived from a plant or medicinal herb. Its pharmacology depends on the plant species, plant part, extraction method and concentration of active constituents, so the standardized extract is the meaningful unit of comparison.\n\nWhat is it used for? Its most relevant biological context in this database is nrf2 / antioxidant response, nf-κb signaling modulation, and microbiome modulation. The current pathway map connects Artichoke Extract with Nrf2 / Antioxidant Response, NF-κB Signaling Modulation, and Microbiome Modulation. For a beginner in pharmacology, these links are useful because they show where an ingredient sits in the chain from molecular interaction to measurable physiology.\n\nHow is it normally taken? There is no single universal dose for this preparation. Human studies use formulation-specific amounts, and the relevant quantity is usually reported for the exact chemical form or extract rather than for the ingredient name alone. Common forms include Whole herb or extract; standardization varies.\n\nThe exact preparation matters: an isolated molecule, a salt, a standardized botanical extract and a multi-ingredient blend should not be treated as equivalent simply because their labels use similar names. The same principle applies to dose: the amount used in a mechanistic experiment may be very different from the amount used in a human supplementation study.\n\nIn SynergyStacks, the ingredient record is intended to be read together with its linked pathways and biomarkers, giving a compact pharmacology map rather than a one-line supplement description.",
