    "description": "Rosemary Extract is a botanical preparation derived from a plant or medicinal herb. Its pharmacology depends on the plant species, plant part, extraction method and concentration of active constituents, so the standardized extract is the meaningful unit of comparison.\n\nIts most relevant biological context in this database is nrf2 / antioxidant response, nf-κb signaling modulation, and microbiome modulation. Common forms include Whole herb or extract; standardization varies. At the biochemical level, the entry is linked to Nrf2 / Antioxidant Response, NF-κB Signaling Modulation, and Microbiome Modulation. These pathways describe the biological processes in which the compound participates; they are not interchangeable with a measured clinical outcome.\n\nWhy it is used: the most common reason to study or supplement Rosemary Extract is to influence the physiology associated with its chemical role, especially nrf2 / antioxidant response, nf-κb signaling modulation, and microbiome modulation. The useful question is therefore not simply whether the ingredient “works”, but which tissue, pathway and measurable outcome are being discussed.\n\nTypical intake: There is no single universal dose for this preparation. Human studies use formulation-specific amounts, and the relevant quantity is usually reported for the exact chemical form or extract rather than for the ingredient name alone.\n\nThis page treats Rosemary Extract as a pharmacology reference entry: chemical identity, formulation, biological role and dose are kept separate so that later research citations can be attached to individual claims rather than to a vague overall statement.",
