    "description": "What is it? Lactoferrin is a protein-based nutritional preparation. Its physiological relevance is determined by amino-acid composition, digestion kinetics and the amount of complete protein delivered to tissues.\n\nWhat is it used for? Its most relevant biological context in this database is mtorc1 signaling. The current pathway map connects Lactoferrin with mTORC1 signaling. For a beginner in pharmacology, these links are useful because they show where an ingredient sits in the chain from molecular interaction to measurable physiology.\n\nHow is it normally taken? Protein supplements are usually measured in grams of protein rather than milligrams of the ingredient. A practical serving commonly supplies about 20–40 g of protein. Common forms include Powder or food-derived preparation.\n\nThe exact preparation matters: an isolated molecule, a salt, a standardized botanical extract and a multi-ingredient blend should not be treated as equivalent simply because their labels use similar names. The same principle applies to dose: the amount used in a mechanistic experiment may be very different from the amount used in a human supplementation study.\n\nIn SynergyStacks, the ingredient record is intended to be read together with its linked pathways and biomarkers, giving a compact pharmacology map rather than a one-line supplement description.",
