    "evidenceLevel": "insufficient",
    "description": "Lactobacillus rhamnosus GG is best understood as a specific chemical entity rather than as a generic “supplement”. Lactobacillus rhamnosus GG is a microbial preparation intended to deliver specific organisms or microbial components to the gastrointestinal tract. Its biological effects are strain- and formulation-dependent, so the organism identity is more informative than the word “probiotic” by itself. In gastrointestinal physiology, it is mainly discussed through digestion, fermentation, intestinal barrier function and host–microbiome interactions.\n\nThe main biological connections currently mapped for this entry are Microbiome Modulation. In practical terms, that means the compound can be studied at several levels: molecular target, pathway activity, downstream biomarker and finally a physiological or clinical outcome. Keeping those levels separate is particularly useful for pharmacology students because a change in one pathway does not automatically imply a change in every downstream endpoint.\n\nForm and dose matter. Common forms include Strain-specific preparation. Typical intake: There is no single universal dose because products are strain-specific; human studies commonly report the amount as colony-forming units (CFU), often in the 10⁹–10¹¹ CFU/day range depending on strain and endpoint.\n\nThe database therefore connects Lactobacillus rhamnosus GG to its mechanisms and biomarkers rather than treating it as an isolated product. That structure makes it possible to compare different forms of the same substance and later attach primary studies to the exact claim being made.",
