    "evidenceLevel": "insufficient",
    "description": "What is it? Sodium Butyrate is a microbial preparation intended to deliver specific organisms or microbial components to the gastrointestinal tract. Its biological effects are strain- and formulation-dependent, so the organism identity is more informative than the word “probiotic” by itself.\n\nWhat is it used for? In gastrointestinal physiology, it is mainly discussed through digestion, fermentation, intestinal barrier function and host–microbiome interactions. The current pathway map connects Sodium Butyrate with Microbiome Modulation. For a beginner in pharmacology, these links are useful because they show where an ingredient sits in the chain from molecular interaction to measurable physiology.\n\nHow is it normally taken? There is no single universal dose because products are strain-specific; human studies commonly report the amount as colony-forming units (CFU), often in the 10⁹–10¹¹ CFU/day range depending on strain and endpoint. Common forms include Strain-specific preparation.\n\nThe exact preparation matters: an isolated molecule, a salt, a standardized botanical extract and a multi-ingredient blend should not be treated as equivalent simply because their labels use similar names. The same principle applies to dose: the amount used in a mechanistic experiment may be very different from the amount used in a human supplementation study.\n\nIn SynergyStacks, the ingredient record is intended to be read together with its linked pathways and biomarkers, giving a compact pharmacology map rather than a one-line supplement description.",
