    "description": "Lactobacillus johnsonii is a microbial preparation intended to deliver specific organisms or microbial components to the gastrointestinal tract. Its biological effects are strain- and formulation-dependent, so the organism identity is more informative than the word “probiotic” by itself.\n\nIn gastrointestinal physiology, it is mainly discussed through digestion, fermentation, intestinal barrier function and host–microbiome interactions. Common forms include Strain-specific preparation. At the biochemical level, the entry is linked to Microbiome Modulation. These pathways describe the biological processes in which the compound participates; they are not interchangeable with a measured clinical outcome.\n\nWhy it is used: the most common reason to study or supplement Lactobacillus johnsonii is to influence the physiology associated with its chemical role, especially microbiome modulation. The useful question is therefore not simply whether the ingredient “works”, but which tissue, pathway and measurable outcome are being discussed.\n\nTypical intake: There is no single universal dose because products are strain-specific; human studies commonly report the amount as colony-forming units (CFU), often in the 10⁹–10¹¹ CFU/day range depending on strain and endpoint.\n\nThis page treats Lactobacillus johnsonii as a pharmacology reference entry: chemical identity, formulation, biological role and dose are kept separate so that later research citations can be attached to individual claims rather than to a vague overall statement.",
