    "description": "Lactase occupies a defined place in human biochemistry. Lactase is an enzyme preparation or enzyme-derived product. Its principal role is catalytic: it accelerates a defined chemical reaction, usually involving digestion, proteolysis, lipid processing or another substrate-specific process.\n\nThe reason it appears in a pharmacology-oriented database is its connection with Lactose digestion. Its most relevant biological context in this database is lactose digestion. This is also why formulation is not a cosmetic detail: Common forms include Lactase.\n\nTypical intake: Enzyme products are dosed by enzymatic activity units rather than simply by milligrams. The usual amount therefore depends on the substrate and the activity specification on the product.\n\nFor study purposes, it is useful to separate four questions. First, what chemical species actually reaches the body? Second, what molecular process can it influence? Third, which biomarker should move if that process changes? Fourth, does that biomarker movement translate into a meaningful physiological outcome? The linked mechanism and biomarker records on this site are designed around exactly that chain.\n\nLater evidence reviews can therefore add studies without rewriting the underlying educational description of Lactase.",
