    "description": "Pepsin is an enzyme preparation or enzyme-derived product. Its principal role is catalytic: it accelerates a defined chemical reaction, usually involving digestion, proteolysis, lipid processing or another substrate-specific process.\n\nIts most relevant biological context in this database is microbiome modulation. Common forms include Pepsin. At the biochemical level, the entry is linked to Microbiome Modulation. These pathways describe the biological processes in which the compound participates; they are not interchangeable with a measured clinical outcome.\n\nWhy it is used: the most common reason to study or supplement Pepsin is to influence the physiology associated with its chemical role, especially microbiome modulation. The useful question is therefore not simply whether the ingredient “works”, but which tissue, pathway and measurable outcome are being discussed.\n\nTypical intake: Enzyme products are dosed by enzymatic activity units rather than simply by milligrams. The usual amount therefore depends on the substrate and the activity specification on the product.\n\nThis page treats Pepsin as a pharmacology reference entry: chemical identity, formulation, biological role and dose are kept separate so that later research citations can be attached to individual claims rather than to a vague overall statement.",
