    "evidenceLevel": "insufficient",
    "description": "Dextrose occupies a defined place in human biochemistry. Dextrose is a sports-nutrition preparation designed to supply a substrate, electrolyte, amino acid or ergogenic compound around exercise. Its role is best understood through the metabolic process it supports rather than the product format alone.\n\nThe reason it appears in a pharmacology-oriented database is its connection with Glycogen Resynthesis. Its most relevant biological context in this database is glycogen resynthesis. This is also why formulation is not a cosmetic detail: Common forms include Dextrose.\n\nTypical intake: Sports protocols commonly provide carbohydrate in the tens of grams per hour during prolonged exercise, with the exact amount matched to exercise duration and intensity.\n\nFor study purposes, it is useful to separate four questions. First, what chemical species actually reaches the body? Second, what molecular process can it influence? Third, which biomarker should move if that process changes? Fourth, does that biomarker movement translate into a meaningful physiological outcome? The linked mechanism and biomarker records on this site are designed around exactly that chain.\n\nLater evidence reviews can therefore add studies without rewriting the underlying educational description of Dextrose.",
