    "description": "MSM occupies a defined place in human biochemistry. MSM is a bioactive compound or formulated ingredient used to study or influence a defined physiological process. Its effects depend on its molecular identity, formulation, exposure and the biological system in which it is evaluated.\n\nThe reason it appears in a pharmacology-oriented database is its connection with NF-κB Signaling Modulation. Its most relevant biological context in this database is nf-κb signaling modulation. This is also why formulation is not a cosmetic detail: Common forms include MSM.\n\nTypical intake: Common supplemental amounts are around 1.5–3 g/day, sometimes divided into two or three doses.\n\nFor study purposes, it is useful to separate four questions. First, what chemical species actually reaches the body? Second, what molecular process can it influence? Third, which biomarker should move if that process changes? Fourth, does that biomarker movement translate into a meaningful physiological outcome? The linked mechanism and biomarker records on this site are designed around exactly that chain.\n\nLater evidence reviews can therefore add studies without rewriting the underlying educational description of MSM.",
