    "description": "GOS is a carbohydrate or fiber substrate that is incompletely digested in the small intestine and can therefore influence stool properties, fermentation and host–microbiome signaling.\n\nA useful pharmacology map for GOS starts with its principal biological associations: Microbiome Modulation. In gastrointestinal physiology, it is mainly discussed through digestion, fermentation, intestinal barrier function and host–microbiome interactions. Those associations place the ingredient in the wider network of metabolism, signaling and measurable physiology rather than presenting it as a standalone claim.\n\nTypical intake: There is no single universal dose for this preparation. Human studies use formulation-specific amounts, and the relevant quantity is usually reported for the exact chemical form or extract rather than for the ingredient name alone.\n\nCommon forms include GOS. Different salts, extracts, delivery systems and combination products can change the amount of active material that reaches the relevant tissue, so the named ingredient should always be read together with its formulation.\n\nFor students, the most useful way to read this record is as a chain: ingredient → mechanism → biomarker → outcome. The database links are intended to make those relationships visible and provide a place where primary research can later be attached to each step.",
