    "description": "What is it? DHEA-S is a bioactive compound or formulated ingredient used to study or influence a defined physiological process. Its effects depend on its molecular identity, formulation, exposure and the biological system in which it is evaluated.\n\nWhat is it used for? Its most relevant biological context in this database is nrf2 / antioxidant response, mitochondrial biogenesis, and autophagy modulation. The current pathway map connects DHEA-S with Nrf2 / Antioxidant Response, Mitochondrial Biogenesis, and Autophagy Modulation. For a beginner in pharmacology, these links are useful because they show where an ingredient sits in the chain from molecular interaction to measurable physiology.\n\nHow is it normally taken? There is no single universal dose for this preparation. Human studies use formulation-specific amounts, and the relevant quantity is usually reported for the exact chemical form or extract rather than for the ingredient name alone. Common forms include DHEA-S.\n\nThe exact preparation matters: an isolated molecule, a salt, a standardized botanical extract and a multi-ingredient blend should not be treated as equivalent simply because their labels use similar names. The same principle applies to dose: the amount used in a mechanistic experiment may be very different from the amount used in a human supplementation study.\n\nIn SynergyStacks, the ingredient record is intended to be read together with its linked pathways and biomarkers, giving a compact pharmacology map rather than a one-line supplement description.",
