    "description": "What is it? Inulin Powder is a carbohydrate or fiber substrate that is incompletely digested in the small intestine and can therefore influence stool properties, fermentation and host–microbiome signaling.\n\nWhat is it used for? In gastrointestinal physiology, it is mainly discussed through digestion, fermentation, intestinal barrier function and host–microbiome interactions. The current pathway map connects Inulin Powder with Microbiome Modulation. For a beginner in pharmacology, these links are useful because they show where an ingredient sits in the chain from molecular interaction to measurable physiology.\n\nHow is it normally taken? Common prebiotic use starts around 3–5 g/day and may be increased toward 10 g/day as tolerated. Common forms include Inulin Powder.\n\nThe exact preparation matters: an isolated molecule, a salt, a standardized botanical extract and a multi-ingredient blend should not be treated as equivalent simply because their labels use similar names. The same principle applies to dose: the amount used in a mechanistic experiment may be very different from the amount used in a human supplementation study.\n\nIn SynergyStacks, the ingredient record is intended to be read together with its linked pathways and biomarkers, giving a compact pharmacology map rather than a one-line supplement description.",
